---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const LAST_UPDATED = '2026-11-24';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Methodology', path: '/methodology/' },
  ]}
  description="How singletenant.ai gathers vendor data, what 'verified' means, the inclusion criteria, and how to submit a correction."
>
  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 md:py-16">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Methodology</p>
      <h1 class="m-0 mb-2" style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        How this data is made
      </h1>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs mb-10" style="color: var(--color-mute);">
        Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}
      </p>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Primary sources only</h2>
        <p>
          Every fact in the vendor database is taken from a primary source:
          the vendor's own documentation, trust centre, legal terms, or
          pricing pages, read at the time of the check. Third-party
          writeups, forum threads, and press coverage are not sources.
          Nothing is filled in from general knowledge or assumption.
        </p>

        <h2>What "verified" means</h2>
        <p>
          A populated fact always carries two things: the URL of the
          primary source it was checked against, and the date of that
          check. "Verified" means the linked page supported the claim on
          that date, not that the claim is true forever. Dates are shown
          per section so staleness is visible rather than hidden.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where a fact could not be confirmed from a primary source, the
          profile shows <strong>"Not verified"</strong>. An honest gap is
          more useful to a procurement decision than a guess.
        </p>

        <h2 id="evidence-levels">Evidence levels</h2>
        <p>
          Certifications and data residency are graded on one rule, applied
          the same way to every vendor:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Contractual</strong>: the commitment is in the vendor's
            data processing agreement, terms or service agreement, or the
            certification can be traced to a certificate, an audit report, or
            the certifying body (for example a downloadable certificate, a
            report available on request, or a regulator's authorisation).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Stated</strong>: the vendor says it on its own product,
            documentation or trust pages, but nothing contractually binding or
            independently traceable backs it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not verified</strong>: nothing was found, or the only
            wording is "aligns with", "designed to" or similar. A certification
            at this level is not listed; data residency shows "Not verified".
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For data residency, Contractual means the agreement lets the customer
          choose where data is stored or processed and commits the vendor to
          it. Stated means the vendor's pages say data stays in a region, or
          document a product control that keeps it there. A HIPAA BAA or GDPR
          DPA counts only when a BAA or DPA is named: "HIPAA compliant" or a
          HIPAA programme alone does not. A certification must cover the
          product being profiled; one held for an unrelated product line is
          not listed. Equivalent wording always gets the same level, whichever
          vendor uses it.
        </p>

        <h2>Inclusion criteria</h2>
        <p>
          The database covers vendors offering single-tenant LLM hosting
          (inference infrastructure where the customer's workload runs on
          capacity not shared with other customers), wherever the vendor is
          based. Category describes how the service is delivered, in three
          kinds: dedicated GPU/inference clouds, hyperscaler dedicated
          capacity, and managed self-host platforms. Geography is never a
          category; it is recorded as parent jurisdiction, regions and
          sovereign for (below). A vendor
          qualifies if it publicly documents a dedicated or single-tenant
          deployment option; the
          <a href="/editorial-policy/#what-qualifies">isolation levels</a>
          the site distinguishes are defined in the editorial policy. Commercial relationships play no part in
          inclusion; see the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a>.
          The rules on sourcing, verification, corrections, and conflicts of
          interest are set out in the
          <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="regions">Regions</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>Regions</strong> column records where a vendor documents
          capacity, not every named data-centre location. A region is added only
          where the vendor's own material names that geography; it is never
          inferred from a neighbouring one. Regions use one controlled
          vocabulary, extended as vendors are added:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Countries</strong>: ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 codes, shown by name
            (for example <code>US</code>, or <code>GB</code> for the United
            Kingdom). A country outside a bloc is never folded into it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Blocs</strong>: a named group of countries treated as one
            data area. <code>EU</code> covers the EU/EEA member states.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Areas</strong>: coarse geographies that carry no law of
            their own: <code>APAC</code> (Asia-Pacific), and
            <code>GLOBAL</code> where the vendor documents worldwide
            availability without a per-region breakdown.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="jurisdiction">Category, jurisdiction and residency</h2>
        <p>
          Four facts are kept apart, because buyers often conflate them:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Category</strong>: how the service is delivered.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Parent jurisdiction</strong>: the country (ISO code) where
            the vendor's parent company is incorporated, from a register or the
            vendor's legal terms. This is the law that can compel the company,
            wherever its data sits.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Data residency</strong>: whether the vendor commits to
            keeping data in a customer-chosen region, graded by the
            <a href="#evidence-levels">evidence levels</a> above.
          </li>
          <li id="sovereign-for">
            <strong>Sovereign for</strong>: recorded for a jurisdiction only
            when all of these hold, each from a primary source: the parent
            company is incorporated there (or in a member state, for a bloc);
            the vendor documents capacity there; data residency is at least
            Stated; and the vendor itself claims sovereignty for that
            jurisdiction for the profiled service. Parent incorporation alone is
            not enough, and the field is never inferred. The schema rejects a
            record where these do not line up.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A region in one country does not change the parent's jurisdiction.
          See the <a href="/jurisdictions/">jurisdictions index</a> for vendors
          and analysis grouped by jurisdiction.
        </p>

        <h2>Update cadence</h2>
        <p>
          The research, writing and updates are done by Claude, an AI model
          made by Anthropic, working to written rules set by the site's owner,
          Simon Newton, who is accountable for what is published (see
          <a href="/editorial-policy/#how-made">how this site is made</a>).
          Since November 2026 Claude also runs the site on a schedule. Each week it checks vendors' own documentation, pricing,
          changelog and trust pages for changes, and every vendor field is
          re-checked against its source at least every 60 days. It applies the
          same rules as above: a fact is published only after its primary
          source has been read, with the source link and the date it was
          checked, and anything it can't confirm stays Not verified. Nothing is
          scraped into the database unread.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every addition, update, and correction is a dated entry in the
          public <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, so you can see exactly
          what changed and when.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          If a fact on this site is wrong or has gone stale, email
          <a href="mailto:simon@singletenant.ai">simon@singletenant.ai</a>
          with the page, the claim, and (if you have it) a link to the
          primary source that contradicts it. We re-check the claim against
          the vendor's published material; if it fails, the data is
          corrected, the verified date is updated, and the correction is
          recorded in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, as set out in
          the <a href="/editorial-policy/#corrections">editorial policy</a>.
          Vendors are
          welcome to submit corrections about their own profiles on the
          same terms: a correction needs a primary source, whoever sends
          it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
